  en: {
    prerequisite: "Complete the web foundations or know how to open the project, edit a file, and verify the result in a browser. No previous commercial experience is required.",
    objective: (title) => `Turn ${title} into concrete, measurable decisions that can be applied to a real store.`,
    mental: (focus) => `${focus} A sound decision always connects the customer need, the visual or commercial rule, its implementation in the site, and an observable signal. This prevents vague preferences and copied tactics whose purpose is not understood.`,
    analogy: (title) => `${title} is like arranging a physical shop: every color, space, message, or offer should help a visitor understand where they are, what they can buy, and why they can trust the next step.`,
    explain1: (focus) => `${focus} Name the precise problem before selecting a tool. Observe context, device, intent, and customer objections. Then write a hypothesis that links one change to an expected result without presenting that hypothesis as established fact.`,
    explain2: (title) => `To master ${title}, first build a minimal version with explicit rules, then inspect it on mobile and desktop. Contrast normal, empty, long, and error cases. Quality comes from a coherent testable system, not from accumulating visual effects or promotional messages.`,
    explain3: (artifact) => `This lesson delivers ${artifact}. It must record the starting situation, decision, expected evidence, and safety limit. Compare behavior and before/after metrics over enough time while accounting for traffic, costs, and satisfaction.`,
    general: (title) => `Apply ${title} to one small page or campaign before scaling it. Document reusable rules, exceptions, and stopping criteria so another person can reproduce the decision without depending on your memory.`,
    lecomax: (artifact) => `In L’ecomax, use this method to produce ${artifact}, then check mobile rendering, accessibility, speed, catalog consistency, and its effect on the journey from discovery to purchase.`,
    expected: (artifact) => `The result is verifiable: ${artifact} exists, its decisions are justified, major edge cases are tested, and at least one measure supports a keep, revise, or stop decision.`,
    symptom1: (title) => `${title} looks attractive, but visitors understand the offer no better and no success criterion exists.`,
    cause1: "Work started from a trend or tool instead of a customer problem, hypothesis, and control signal.",
    fix1: (title) => `Restate the ${title} problem, choose one priority change, define expected evidence, and test mobile, keyboard, and long-content cases.`,
    symptom2: (title) => `Numbers associated with ${title} rise while margin, returns, unsubscribes, or satisfaction deteriorate.`,
    cause2: "A local metric was optimized without guardrails or a view of total cost and acquired-customer quality.",
    fix2: "Add margin, refund, retention, consent, and satisfaction criteria; stop the experiment when a guardrail crosses its limit.",
    task: (artifact) => `Build ${artifact} from the template, apply it to one L’ecomax product, and state the evidence required before publication.`,
    steps: ["Describe the customer, intent, main objection, and current state with evidence.", "Complete every template decision without copying a competitor or inventing data.", "Test on mobile and desktop, then record result, risk, metric, and next action."],
    hints: ["Narrow the scope to one product, one audience, and one primary action.", "When two options look good, choose the one that improves understanding while protecting trust, accessibility, and margin."],
    solution: (title, artifact) => `The reference solution for ${title} completes every section of ${artifact}, separates facts from hypotheses, adds a primary measure and guardrails, and requires real verification before scaling.`,
    quiz: (title) => `Which approach correctly applies ${title}?`,
    options: ["Copy a competitor’s most visible tactic and publish it everywhere.", "Define the problem, hypothesis, small test, primary measure, and guardrails before scaling.", "Add more colors, promotions, and popups to attract attention.", "Look only at click count for a few hours."],
    quizExplain: (title) => `For ${title}, the correct answer connects a real problem to a bounded experiment and multiple forms of evidence; it does not confuse attention, causality, and durable business results.`,
    recap: (title, artifact) => [`${title} starts from an observable customer problem, never an isolated trend.`, `The ${artifact} deliverable makes hypotheses, decisions, risks, and evidence shareable.`, "An improvement is kept only when the primary result, margin, trust, and satisfaction remain compatible."],
    next: (title, nextTitle) => `After ${title}, continue with ${nextTitle} to connect the new skill to the next part of the selling system.`,
    manual: "Manually review the template with a real product and a narrow mobile viewport",
  },
